  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1138 AM MDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nCOOLER AND WETTER PATTERN THIS WEEK: \n\nResidual moisture and daytime heating will result in terrain-driven \nshowers and storms this afternoon and evening. The jet associated \nwith a low pressure in the PacNW has spread over the region. This \nmeans there is some shear present, which could support a few \nstronger storms, especially across the northwest half of the CWA. \nThis evening there should be a transition to more stratiform with \nembedded storms. \n\nAs that low pressure passes to our north southwest flow will advect \na pocket of deep moisture into the area tonight. PWAT values peak \nover 200 percent. Therefore expect the coverage of precipitation to \nincrease during this surge. The high-res models focus showers north \nof I-70 early in the night then south of I-70 late in the night, \nwhich may be due to upper level jet forcing and a weak front. \nPeriods of moderate to heavy rain are possible and could result\nin localized flooding, rockfalls and mudslides. Widespread \nshowers are expected south of I-70 tomorrow morning, while the \nnorth starts clearing out. The moisture may somewhat decrease, but\nit will still be sufficient for redevelopment through out the day.\nThe intensity and coverage will depend on the instability. So \nplaces that clear out have higher chances of storms in the \nafternoon. The jet will still be overhead so some storms may \nbecome strong to severe.\n\nThe area remains in southwest flow aloft as the first low pressure \ntracks over the Midwest and another low drops into the PacNW. \nThis will ultimately keep the moisture in place through at least\nmid week, so expect the chances for showers and storms to \ncontinue. On Wednesday the models are hinting that a subtle wave\nmoves in from the southwest. If this were to happen it would \ncertainly be a focus for showers and storms especially given the\nmoisture will still be around. Late in the week, the models are\nnow agreeing that the second low pressure cuts off in the PacNW\nand northern Great Basin. Model performance is typical poor for\nthese cut off lows, mainly how long they stick around. This \nlowers confidence in the forecast late in the period. As it \nstands now though this period looks to be active due to the low \npressure and moisture.",
